## Steps and evidence

### D0 -- baseline, feature OFF
  10.38.22.65 -> 10.38.22.70 : 0% packet loss
  10.38.22.66 -> 10.38.22.70 : 0% packet loss
  PASS. Both sources reach the far side before any ACL exists.

### NEGATIVE CONTROL -- an ingress ACL on a LAG does not touch traffic that only EGRESSES it
  x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list hardware t44992-lag
  x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
  x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface sa1
  x960-56YSQ(config-if)#access-group t44992-lag
  show access-list       -> Hardware IP access list t44992-lag
                            4 deny ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
  show running-config interface sa1 -> access-group t44992-lag  (bound)
  show platform classifier statistics utilization brief -> ACL 0 -> 1, Total 2 -> 3 / 1024
  show access-list counters -> Packet Hits: 0
  pings: .65 0% loss, .66 0% loss   (NOT denied)
  This is CORRECT, not a defect. `access-group` is an INGRESS filter. host->swi_b traffic
  ingresses port1.0.48 and EGRESSES sa1, so it never meets an ingress ACL on sa1; the reply
  (src .70 -> dst .65) does ingress sa1 but does not match a rule written src .65 -> dst .70.
  The rule was nevertheless PROGRAMMED (TCAM ACL 0 -> 1), so the binding itself works.
  Kept as the negative control. The sentinel independently flagged the same reasoning.

### STEP 1 -- D1 positive proof, host and host (match the flow that ingresses the LAG)
  x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#no deny ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
  x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.65/32
  show access-list -> 4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.65/32
  10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss      DENIED
  10.38.22.66 ->   0% packet loss      PERMITTED (non-matching, implicit permit)
  show access-list counters -> Packet Hits: 4   (exactly the 4 denied echo replies)
  PASS. Matching traffic denied, non-matching permitted, counter corroborates.

### STEP 2 -- effective on EVERY member of the static LAG   (the heart of AWP-8659)
  A) DUT interface port1.0.2 shutdown  -> traffic can only use port1.0.16
     show static-channel-group -> Member: port1.0.2, port1.0.16
     .65 100% loss (DENIED)   .66 0% loss (PERMITTED)   hits 4 -> 10
  B) DUT port1.0.2 no shutdown ; port1.0.16 shutdown -> traffic can only use port1.0.2
     .65 100% loss (DENIED)   .66 0% loss (PERMITTED)   hits 10 -> 13
  C) port1.0.16 no shutdown -> Member: port1.0.2, port1.0.16 restored
  PASS. One ACL bound to the aggregator enforces on BOTH members independently.

### STEP 3 -- D4, non-IP traffic unaffected
  ip neigh flush dev eth4 ; ping .65 -> .70 (denied)
  ip neigh show dev eth4 -> 10.38.22.70 lladdr 00:00:cd:42:30:29 REACHABLE
  PASS. ARP re-resolved through the LAG while ICMP was denied; an IP ACL does not touch ARP.

### STEP 4 -- first match wins, evaluated top to bottom
  x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#1 permit ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.65/32
  show access-list -> 1 permit ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.65/32
                      4 deny   ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.65/32
  .65 -> 0% packet loss      PERMITTED -- the lower-sequence permit wins over the deny
  x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#no 1
  show access-list -> 4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.65/32   (permit gone)
  .65 -> 100% packet loss    DENIED again
  PASS. Ordering is first-match; removal by sequence number works.

### STEP 5 -- network (prefix) address form, src host + dst network
  4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.64/27
  .65 -> 100% loss   .66 -> 100% loss     both DENIED (both are inside 10.38.22.64/27)
  PASS. Host and network address forms both select correctly.

### STEP 6 -- vlan qualifier, with and without
  4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.64/27 vlan 100   (MATCHING vlan)
     .65 -> 100% packet loss    DENIED
  4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.64/27 vlan 1     (NON-matching vlan)
     .65 ->   0% packet loss    PERMITTED
  PASS. The vlan qualifier narrows the match correctly in both directions.

### STEP 7 -- the same ACL via service policy  (TRAP 1 observed in full)
  Trap 1: interface ACLs are matched BEFORE QoS, so the access-group had to be removed and
  PROVEN gone first, or the QoS path would never have been exercised.
  a) rule set back to 4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.64/27
     x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface sa1 ; no access-group t44992-lag
     show running-config interface sa1 -> switchport / switchport mode access /
                                          switchport access vlan 100     NO access-group
     show access-list -> the ACL still exists
     .65 -> 0% packet loss    PROOF the ACL path is no longer in effect
  b) x960-56YSQ(config)#mls qos enable
     -> "Traffic will stop while configuration is applied. Continue? (y/n): " answered y
     class-map t44992-cm ; match access-group t44992-lag
     policy-map t44992-pm ; class t44992-cm
     interface sa1 ; service-policy input t44992-pm
     show running-config interface sa1 -> service-policy input t44992-pm  (and no access-group)
  c) with 4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.64/27 : .65 100% loss, .66 100% loss
     (both correct -- /27 covers both)
     rule narrowed to 4 deny ip 10.38.22.70/32 10.38.22.65/32 for a true control:
     .65 -> 100% packet loss    DENIED
     .66 ->   0% packet loss    PERMITTED
  PASS. The same named hardware ACL enforces on the aggregator through the QoS service-policy
  path as well as through access-group.

