        == exactly the pre-case baseline
  .65 -> 0% packet loss    traffic restored
  PASS. Clean removal, no residue in config or in TCAM.

## TCAM measurements  (Test Strategy's named risk: the 2.5K Falcon rule budget)

  state                                           ACL  QoS  Total / pool
  pre-case baseline (Loop Detection only)           0    0     2 / 1024  0.20%
  1 rule bound with `access-group` on sa1           1    0     3 / 1024  0.29%
  SAME 1 rule bound via `service-policy input`      0    3     5 / 1024  0.49%
  after full teardown                               0    0     2 / 1024  0.20%
  Egress pool unchanged throughout: 0 / 6144. No "Rule Limit Reached" counter moved.

  FINDING F5: the SAME logical rule costs 1 ingress entry via access-group but 3 via the QoS
  service-policy path -- a 3x multiplier. Against the measured 1024-entry ingress pool (NOT
  the 2.5K-3K the TPS and platforms/x960.md section 2 describe -- see the triage report), this
  is directly material to the Test Strategy's "TCAM utilisation and contention with QoS"
  coverage area and its named rule-budget risk.
  FINDING F6: `show access-list counters` reports Packet Hits 0 while the ACL is bound via
  service-policy, although the deny is demonstrably enforced. Hit counters appear to work only
  on the access-group path. Anyone measuring a QoS-bound ACL by its ACL counters would read a
  working rule as dead.

## Findings (platform / method)

F1  `% This access-list is attached to an interface. The modified configuration will be applied
    to the interface automatically after exiting this configuration mode by entering "exit" or
    "end" command.` is an ADVISORY, not a rejection -- it is printed on ENTERING the sub-mode of
    an ACL that is bound to an interface, and the command succeeds. tools/cfg.py (stop-on-error)
    ABORTED on it and silently skipped the rest of the batch; I only caught it because the
    following ping did not change. This is a SECOND advisory of the kind platforms/x960.md
    section 5 already records for the mirror port. Workaround used: ckcon.py without
    --stop-on-error. cfg.py needs this string on an allow-list.
F2  `no mls qos enable` -> `% Invalid input detected at '^' marker.` The correct negation is
    `no mls qos`. Note the asymmetry: `mls qos enable` PROMPTS (y/n), `no mls qos` does NOT.
F3  `copy flash:/precampaign.cfg flash:/default.cfg` ->
    `% Cannot overwrite flash:/default.cfg as it is the default config file`. The active boot
    config cannot be restored by a direct copy. Restored instead by re-saving the torn-down
    running-config (see Restore).
F4  The named hardware ACL sub-mode offers `mac` under deny/permit; the numbered
    `access-list 3000 ?` form does not.
F5  TCAM cost 1 (access-group) vs 3 (service-policy) for the same rule -- see above.
F6  ACL hit counters do not increment on the service-policy path -- see above.
F7  METHOD: an ingress `access-group` on an aggregator filters only traffic INGRESSING the
    aggregator. A case that sources traffic from the host side must write the rule in the
    return direction (or source from the far side). Grading the no-match case as "ACL
    ineffective on a LAG" would be a method error, not a product finding.
F8  HOST: Linux dropped the secondary address 10.38.22.66/27 from eth4 when the link bounced
    during the DUT reload. Re-add it after any reload before using it as a control.

